            <ArticleTitle>Dilated coronary arterial obstruction due to coronary artery microfistulae.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>417-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jcct.2013.11.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1934-5925(13)00474-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An 80-year-old asymptomatic man presented with ST-segment elevation in leads V1 to V5. Coronary CT angiography showed that microfistulae arising from multiple arteries may have led to myocardial infarction from intracoronary thromboembolism within the dilated left anterior descending (LAD).</AbstractText>
